In the long-term strategy, users gain incentive to return products when remaining value is recognized and compensated. The benefit of this configuration for the manufacturer always is direct access to used products of known history and controlled condition. Buyback programs establish closed material loops where manufacturers control refurbishment quality and output channels.

Direct product recovery enables manufacturers to capture residual product value rather than losing it to informal second-hand markets. Historical product data collected through buyback enables accelerated remanufacturing and improved next-generation designs. Buyback programs create market conditions where product lifespan becomes manufacturer responsibility rather than user choice.